speechtext:
Two very vicious principles have been enunciated by the Minister of Inland Revenue in his brief remarks. First, he says that he is not ashamed to ask for this vote because during the last six years not $50 has been expended in his county. Second, he says that the people want this appropriation. Of course they want it. That is what I have been trying to fight against for some years. The people " want '-and as long as they have a paternal government that will give them what they want, they will continue to make demands. No man knows that better than the Minister of Inland Revenue. He stated that no public money has been expended in his constituency. Well, no public money has been expended in my constituency during the last six years, unless for necessary repairs to the three public buildings that we have in that county. There has been no new work. I have not asked for any;
I would not ask for anything unless it was absolutely necessary. Although I do not represent a constituency of saints, I will never go to my constituents and ask them to vote against the Government because public money has not been spent in the constituency. No public money was spent ' in my constituency when I supported the Government; I could never ask them to vote for me on that account. The acting Minister of Public Works says something about the St. John Valley railway. Yes, the Government gave a subsidy to the St! John Valley railway, and their political friends stole $100,000 at the very first crack," in one lump, a few days before the provin-[Mr. A. K. Maclean.!
cial election of 1912. I do not want public money sim.ply because of a desire that public money shall be expended. I commence to see light in this matter. The Minister of Agriculture (Mr. Burrell) sat here all last evening and all to-day; his colleagues from his own province were here, waiting and wondering when these infernal Grits would ever stop talking. The Minister of Agriculture has $200,000 in the estimates for telephones; his colleague, the. Minister of Inland Revenue, had one hundred bulls in his constituency after the last election. As Gadsby says, he converted a lot of them into benevolent neutrals, in order that they might be more docile in the yoke, and in order that they might work in the fields. When I see these two hon. gentlemen working together, not for the propagation of live stock, but for the prevention of the propagation of live stock-well, the plot thickens. The conspiracy is so absolutely patent that I think we should have some explanation from the Minister of Agriculture.

I have no doubt that the Minister of Agiiculture is right in his desire that these people should have telephone and telegraphic communication with the outside world. I appreciate their necessity; I myself, if I lived in a place where I had no communication with the outside world, would be glad to be furnished with telephone communication. However, these people have been there since a little after 1849, at the time of the California gold craze; they have done without telephone and telegraph communication for a great number of years, and I suppose they got along very well. That was a very touching story that we heard about the man with the sick child; I always feel sympathy for people who are so situated. At the same time, when these people went into the country of their own free will, there was no telephone ahead of them, no telegraph, no railway, in some cases no roads. Last year we bought a railway because those who had lived in the locality in question for a hundred and fifty years had never had railway communication. These people are in somewhat the same box. During three years Canada has experienced condi-[Mr. Carvell.l
tions of financial distress; the people are being taxed very heavily for the carrying on of the war. They do not resent that, but they do resent the undertaking of new expenditure not connected with the war. I do not say that these expenditures are being undertaken for election purposes;
I do not know that that is true. But I do say that these people could get along very well without this accommodation until after the war is over. A few linkings up might have been provided for, but I think it is a shoit-sighted and uncalled for policy on the part of the Government to spend about $200,000 at a time when we require every dollar we, can get for our part in the war. When the main estimates were going through the then Minister of Public Works (Mr. Rogers) told us that no new undertakings would be proceeded with this year, or during the war. We took his word for that, and passed his estimates a3 they came up. He was good enough to strike off many of the estimates that had been in for two or three years, in order to demonstrate his intention of really not undertaking any expenditure that could be avoided. As it is the intention of the Government to put these items through, no matter what we say about them, I suppose it is a waste of time to discuss them. However, I cannot but protest against this unnecessary expenditure during the war.
